    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>A sofa is one of the biggest furniture decisions in a home, and pets change the brief completely. Claws snag loops, fur clings to certain textures, and muddy paws and the occasional accident test any cleaning claims. No upholstery is truly scratch-proof, but some fabrics cope far better than others.</p>
<p>This guide compares the main options, including the common question of performance fabric vs leather with dogs, and explains how to read rub-test ratings and cleaning codes so you can choose a sofa that still looks good after years of family life.</p>

<h2 id="what-to-look-for">What makes a fabric pet-friendly</h2>
<p>Whatever the fibre, the same few qualities decide how a sofa fabric copes with pets:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>A tight, flat weave.</strong> The fewer loops and gaps on the surface, the less there is for a claw to hook into. Run a fingernail across a sample: if it catches, a claw will too.</li>
<li><strong>Durability.</strong> A good rub-test rating suggests the fabric will resist wear from constant use (more on this below).</li>
<li><strong>Cleanability.</strong> Can you wipe it, spot-clean it with water, or remove the covers and wash them?</li>
<li><strong>Fur release.</strong> Smooth, tightly woven surfaces let hair be brushed or vacuumed off; textured and napped fabrics tend to grip it.</li>
<li><strong>Colour and pattern</strong> that disguise hair and everyday marks.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="fabrics-that-cope-well">Fabrics that tend to cope well</h2>
<h3 id="microfibre">Microfibre and microsuede</h3>
<p>Microfibre is made from very fine synthetic fibres packed into a dense, tight surface. Because there are no loops, claws are less likely to snag it, and fur can usually be lifted with a damp cloth, lint roller or upholstery brush. Many microfibres are water-cleanable, but always check the care label. Microsuede can show “tracks” and watermarks, so test a hidden area before cleaning.</p>
<h3 id="performance-fabrics">Performance fabrics</h3>
<p>“Performance fabric” is a broad term for upholstery engineered to resist stains, moisture and wear. Some are made from solution-dyed synthetic fibres, where the colour runs through the fibre itself; others rely on a stain-resistant finish. Most have a tight weave and a good durability rating, which makes them a strong all-round choice for dogs and cats.</p>
<p>Because the term is used loosely, ask for specifics: what the fibre content is, whether stain resistance comes from the fibre or a coating, the rub-test rating, and the recommended cleaning method.</p>
<h3 id="canvas-and-denim">Canvas, denim and tightly woven cotton blends</h3>
<p>Heavy, tightly woven canvas, cotton drill and denim are hard-wearing and relaxed in look. They are often used for loose, washable covers, which is a real advantage with pets. Pure cotton can stain and may shrink if washed incorrectly, so check whether the covers are pre-shrunk and follow the maker’s instructions.</p>

<h2 id="fabrics-to-avoid">Fabrics to avoid or think twice about</h2>
<!--figure:living-room-curved-sofa-sculptural-chair|A rust boucle chair beside a curved sofa: looped textures like boucle are beautiful but easy for claws to catch.-->
<ul>
<li><strong>Boucle and other looped textures:</strong> the loops are exactly what claws hook into, and pulled loops are hard to repair.</li>
<li><strong>Loose or open weaves</strong>, including chunky slubby linens and basket weaves: easy to snag and slow to release fur.</li>
<li><strong>Velvet with a looped pile</strong> and very soft, napped fabrics: they can attract and hold hair, and claws can mark the pile. Some tightly woven, cut-pile performance velvets resist snags better, so ask for a sample to test.</li>
<li><strong>Delicate silks and fine linens:</strong> prone to water marks and damage.</li>
<li><strong>Chenille:</strong> its soft, raised yarns snag easily.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you love one of these, consider using it on a less-used accent chair or cushions and choosing a tougher fabric for the main sofa.</p>

<h2 id="performance-fabric-vs-leather">Performance fabric vs leather with dogs</h2>
<p>Leather is often recommended for pet owners because fur does not cling to it and spills wipe away. Whether it suits you depends on the type of leather and how you feel about marks.</p>
<h3 id="leather-types">Not all leather is the same</h3>
<!--figure:study-walnut-shelving-armchair|A leather armchair in a home study: quality leather will show marks with use but develops a soft patina over time.-->
<ul>
<li><strong>Full-grain and top-grain leather</strong> are the more durable grades. Claws can still scratch and occasionally puncture them, but light marks often blend in as the leather develops a patina, and many can be buffed or treated with a conditioner suitable for that leather.</li>
<li><strong>Aniline and semi-aniline</strong> (natural-looking, lightly finished) leathers are soft and beautiful but show scratches and absorb stains more readily.</li>
<li><strong>Pigmented or protected leather</strong> has a surface coating that resists stains and light scuffs better, though deep scratches can still show through the colour.</li>
<li><strong>Bonded leather</strong> is made from leather fibres bonded to a backing. It can peel or crack with wear and is generally a poor choice for pets.</li>
<li><strong>Faux leather</strong> (polyurethane or PVC) is easy to wipe but can be punctured by claws and may peel over time; quality varies widely.</li>
</ul>
<h3 id="how-they-compare">How they compare</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Fur:</strong> leather wins; hair simply wipes or vacuums off. Tight performance weaves come a close second.</li>
<li><strong>Scratches:</strong> a tight-weave performance fabric or microfibre usually hides minor claw contact better than smooth leather, which records marks. If you like a worn, lived-in look, scratched top-grain leather can be charming; if you want it to look new, it may frustrate you.</li>
<li><strong>Accidents and spills:</strong> protected leather and many performance fabrics are easy to wipe if dealt with quickly. Liquid can still seep into seams and cushions on either, so act fast.</li>
<li><strong>Comfort:</strong> leather can feel cool in winter and sticky in summer; fabric feels more consistent and is often preferred by dogs who like to curl up.</li>
<li><strong>Odour:</strong> smooth leather tends not to hold smells; fabric benefits from removable, washable covers.</li>
</ul>
<p>For large or boisterous dogs with strong claws, a tight-weave performance fabric with removable covers is often the most forgiving. For short-haired dogs that shed heavily but are gentle on furniture, protected leather can work well.</p>

<h2 id="rub-tests-explained">Rub tests explained</h2>
<p>Upholstery durability is usually given as a rub count. In the UK and Europe, the Martindale test rubs the fabric in a figure-of-eight pattern until it shows visible wear; in the US, the Wyzenbeek test measures “double rubs” in a back-and-forth motion. The two methods are different, so their numbers cannot be compared directly.</p>
<p>As a rough guide to Martindale ratings, fabrics are commonly described as suitable for general domestic use from around 15,000 rubs and for heavy domestic use from around 25,000 rubs upwards. For a family sofa with pets, aim for a fabric sold as heavy-duty domestic or higher. Remember that a rub count measures abrasion, not snag resistance: a high-rated fabric with a loose, loopy surface can still catch on claws.</p>

<h2 id="cleaning-codes">Cleaning codes</h2>
<p>Many upholstery fabrics carry a cleaning code on the label or sample, especially those made for international markets:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>W:</strong> can be cleaned with water-based cleaners.</li>
<li><strong>S:</strong> solvent-based cleaner only; water may mark it.</li>
<li><strong>W/S or SW:</strong> either water-based or solvent-based cleaners can be used.</li>
<li><strong>X:</strong> vacuum or brush only; no liquid cleaners.</li>
</ul>
<p>For pets, a W or W/S fabric is the most practical, and an X fabric is best avoided. Always follow the manufacturer’s care instructions and test any cleaner on a hidden area first.</p>
<blockquote><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Ask for a large sample and live with it for a week: brush it with a fingernail, rub fur onto it, spill a little water on it and see how it cleans before committing to a whole sofa.</p></blockquote>

<h2 id="colour-and-covers">Colour, pattern and removable covers</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Match the colour to your pet’s coat</strong> where you can. Mid-tones such as taupe, mushroom, warm grey and oatmeal are forgiving for many coats; very dark sofas show light fur, and pale sofas show dark fur and muddy paws.</li>
<li><strong>Subtle pattern and texture</strong>, such as a tight herringbone, flecked weave or small-scale tweed-effect, disguises hair and marks better than a flat, solid colour, provided the weave itself is tight.</li>
<li><strong>Removable, washable covers</strong> make a huge difference. Check whether the seat cushions, back cushions and arms all unzip, and whether the covers can be washed at home or need professional cleaning.</li>
<li><strong>Throws and fitted covers</strong> on your pet’s favourite spot protect the sofa and can be washed regularly.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="protecting-your-sofa">Other ways to protect your sofa</h2>
<ul>
<li>Give cats a sturdy scratching post near the sofa and dogs a comfortable bed of their own nearby.</li>
<li>Keep claws trimmed, following your vet’s or groomer’s advice.</li>
<li>Vacuum the sofa weekly with an upholstery attachment to stop fur and grit working into the fabric.</li>
<li>Choose seat cushions with sewn-in or reversible covers so you can rotate them and spread wear.</li>
</ul>

